sections.s2_technical:
The persistence context holds a strong reference to every managed entity plus a snapshot of its loaded state for dirty checking. In a bulk insert loop, each `save()` adds a managed entity (and queues an insert), and because the transaction stays open, nothing is released. For a million entities you hold a million objects and a million snapshots, so heap usage climbs until the JVM dies with `OutOfMemoryError`. A secondary effect is that dirty checking at flush must scan every managed entity, which gets slower as the context grows.

```java
// NAIVE — memory balloons, OOM for large N
@Transactional
public void naiveLoad(List<AccountData> data) {
    for (AccountData d : data) {
        accountRepo.save(new Account(d));  // every entity stays managed → heap grows
    }
}
```

The flush+clear pattern bounds the context:

```java
@Transactional
public void batchLoad(List<AccountData> data) {
    int i = 0;
    for (AccountData d : data) {
        em.persist(new Account(d));
        if (++i % BATCH == 0) {
            em.flush();   // send this batch's INSERTs to the DB
            em.clear();   // detach everything → free memory
        }
    }
    em.flush();  // final partial batch
}
```

`flush()` executes the buffered inserts for the current batch; `clear()` detaches all managed entities so the garbage collector reclaims them. After clear, the context is empty and the next batch starts fresh, so the resident set stays around N entities instead of growing.

To make the flushed inserts efficient, you combine this with JDBC batching: set `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size` to match (or be a divisor of) your N, set `order_inserts` and `order_updates` to true so same-table statements group into JDBC batches, and avoid `IDENTITY` id generation because it forces an immediate insert per row and disables batching (use `SEQUENCE` with a matching allocation size).

Senior nuances: choose N to balance memory against round trips (commonly 50–1000). The whole loop is still one transaction, so a failure rolls back everything flushed so far; for very large loads consider committing per batch (separate transactions) to avoid a huge rollback and long lock holding, accepting that partial progress persists. Also disable second-level cache for the batch, clear the context's event listeners overhead, and if using Spring Data, note `saveAll` does not magically batch — you still need the config and often manual flush/clear for truly large loads. Finally, after `clear`, any entity reference you kept is detached, so do not touch lazy associations on it.

